    "topic_title": "Rational Functions and End Behavior",
    "stem": "For f(x) = (6x²−5x+2)/(3x²+7x−4), what value does f(x) approach as x becomes arbitrarily large in either the positive or negative direction?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "1/2",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reversed-ratio error: this divides the denominator’s leading coefficient by the numerator’s. Because f(x) is numerator divided by denominator, the relevant ratio is 6/3, not 3/6. Fix: Write the leading-coefficient ratio in numerator-over-denominator order."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "2",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The numerator and denominator both have degree 2, so their leading terms determine the end behavior. For large positive or negative x, the ratio approaches 6x²/(3x²) = 2."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "−1/2",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Constant-term error: 2/(−4) gives f(0), not the end behavior. For large input magnitudes, the quadratic terms dominate the constant terms. Fix: Identify the highest-degree term in each polynomial before forming the ratio."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "0",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Denominator-growth error: a growing denominator does not force the quotient toward zero when the numerator grows at the same degree. Here, both polynomials have degree 2. Fix: Compare both degrees before deciding whether the quotient approaches zero."
     }
    ]
   },
